## Inbound (Server) Timeouts

```properties
# How long Tomcat waits for client to send HTTP request data
server.tomcat.connection-timeout=60000  # 60 seconds, default

# Keep-alive connections: Tomcat 9.0+
server.tomcat.keep-alive-timeout=60000
server.tomcat.max-keep-alive-requests=100
```

## Outbound (Client) Timeouts — RestTemplate

```java
@Bean
public RestTemplate restTemplate() {
    HttpComponentsClientHttpRequestFactory factory =
        new HttpComponentsClientHttpRequestFactory();
    factory.setConnectTimeout(3000);   // Connect to server: 3s
    factory.setReadTimeout(10000);     // Read response: 10s
    return new RestTemplate(factory);
}
```

## Outbound (Client) Timeouts — WebClient

```java
@Bean
public WebClient webClient() {
    HttpClient httpClient = HttpClient.create()
        .option(ChannelOption.CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MILLIS, 3000)
        .responseTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10));
    return WebClient.builder()
        .clientConnector(new ReactorClientHttpConnector(httpClient))
        .build();
}
```

## Production Reality

Always set read timeouts on outbound HTTP clients. A sensible starting point: 3 seconds connect timeout, 10-30 seconds read timeout depending on the downstream SLA. Combine with a circuit breaker (Resilience4j) so repeated timeouts to a slow service don't keep blocking your threads.
